{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And it was, the one who offered on the first day, etc.</b> This passage is joined to the priestly blessing, whose seal of all the blessings is peace — for if there is no peace, there is nothing. Therefore peace comes at the conclusion of the seal, and the last is most beloved, for all the earlier ones appear as though they are still lacking until the arrival of the blessing that comes after them to fill what the first blessing lacked; but the last one lacks nothing, for there is no further need for any blessing to come after it to fill its deficiency. Therefore the midrash praises and says: Great is peace, for it is the seal of all the blessings — for this demonstrates the deficiency of the earlier blessings and the excellence of the seal, and the creation of man on the last [day] will prove it. And therefore many midrashim are found that speak of the excellence of peace, which is needed among those below and among those on high. And they said further (Bemidbar Rabbah 11:7): Great is peace, for even the dead need it, as it says (Genesis 15:15), ״וְאַתָּה תָּבוֹא אֶל אֲבֹתֶיךָ בְּשָׁלוֹם״ — the meaning is not that there is some quarrel and strife among the dead such that they would need peace; rather, the dead need there to be peace among the living, for if there is no peace among the living and they insult one another, his word will quickly run to insult the living and the dead, until even for those who dwell in the dust there is no rest in the grave from the quarrelling tongues of the living — as in the story of that woman who was buried in a mat of reeds (Berakhot 18b), and as happens every day in our generation. Therefore the dead need there to be peace among the living, and then they will rest upon their beds in peace." "And after he mentioned the seal of all the blessings, which is peace, he immediately began with a passage in which peace is likewise alluded to, for this reason it is said of the first one, ״וְקָרְבָּנוֹ״ — with a vav of inclusion, as though others had preceded him, so that his glory not be to say, 'I offered first.' Also the title נָשִׂיא (prince) is not mentioned for him, so that he not exalt himself to say, 'I shall rule' — and pride is the cause of every quarrel and every plague. And they said further (Shemot Rabbah 15:6, see there) that all these twelve tribes are likened to the twelve constellations, which proceed like one who walks backward on a ladder, and so the poet of the wedding hymn established: 'an ox before them and not behind them, ascending in peace' — for each one thinks, 'I am first.' And for this reason the Torah lengthened its mention of all the offerings of all twelve princes, so as not to make any one subordinate to another, and all this is for the sake of peace."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "The citation במ״ר יא ז resolved as Bemidbar Rabbah 11:7 based on standard abbreviation; the story reference ברכות יח: resolved as Berakhot 18b.", 1 "Citation שמו״ר טו ו resolved as Shemot Rabbah 15:6; the phrase עיי״ש rendered as 'see there.' The wedding hymn reference is to the piyyut 'שור לפניהם' sung at bridal processions."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:39:47.377260037Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}